---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { renderInline, slug } from '../../lib/md';

// Module cards come from the stdlib catalog; only the section framing
// prose lives here.
const modules = (await getCollection('stdlib')).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---

<Docs
  title="std"
  description="The modules and functions available to VL programs."
  eyebrow="Reference"
  availability="VL 0.1+"
>
  <h1 id="std">std</h1>
  <p>
    The standard library is the set of modules a VL program can import. Start with
    the <a href="/docs">learning path</a> if you are new to the language, then use these pages
    to look up a function or open the VL source behind a module.
  </p>
  <h2 id="modules">Modules</h2>
  {
    modules.map((entry) => (
      <Fragment>
        <h3 id={slug(entry.data.module)}>
          <a href={`/std/${entry.id}`}>
            <code>{entry.data.module}</code>
          </a>
        </h3>
        <p><Fragment set:html={renderInline(entry.data.summary)} /></p>
      </Fragment>
    ))
  }
  <h2 id="availability">Availability</h2>
  <p>
    Native functions are supplied by the target. Helpers written in VL are
    embedded into the compiled program when used; modules such as
    <code>std.ascii</code>, <code>std.array</code>, <code>std.parse</code>, and <code>std.path</code>
    build on language operations and do not need dedicated VM calls.
  </p>
  <p>
    `std`, `std.ascii`, `std.array`, `std.parse`, `std.path`, `std.string`,
    `std.math`, and `std.fmt` work everywhere, including the browser playground
    (wasm32-freestanding). `std.fs` and `std.net.tcp`
    are native-only: available when running on linux-x86_64, not available
    in the playground (freestanding WASM hosts do not register filesystem
    or socket support).
  </p>
  <h2 id="runtime-work">Runtime work for a broader standard library</h2>
  <p>
    Naravm already provides printing, byte-string operations, unsigned decimal
    and hexadecimal conversion, modulo, file reading, TCP sockets, process
    arguments, and process exit. VL binds only the functions listed on these
    pages. For example, <code>process.argv</code> and <code>process.exit</code>
    exist in the VM but still need VL native signatures and backend mappings
    before VL programs can call them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Additional VM capabilities for a wider standard library include stdin,
    general file open/read/write/close and directory or metadata operations,
    a clock, and a random source. The existing stdout write is byte-at-a-time
    and lacks a typed failure result. `std.string.to_bytes` currently copies
    through checked byte reads; a length-aware bridge over the raw
    string/container conversion natives would make array-to-string conversion
    practical and safe. Recoverable allocation failures and native status
    codes would also support typed errors instead of traps.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some work is in VL or the compiler rather than the VM: `Array.new` and
    copying can build growable collections, while a native resize operation
    would make growth cheaper. Generic object support is needed for an
    ergonomic `List[T]`, and first-class callbacks would enable APIs such as
    map/filter. Unicode decoding and validation can be written over current
    byte access; VM support would make character-aware operations faster.
    Numeric ordering for `f64` also needs backend lowering before generic
    extrema and sort can use it on Naravm.
  </p>
</Docs>
